WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.390 --> 00:00:04.520
Hong Kong is 92% ethnically Chinese.

00:00:04.520 --> 00:00:09.200
But stroll through the central business district
on any given Sunday and you wouldn’t know

00:00:09.200 --> 00:00:10.200
it.

00:00:10.200 --> 00:00:15.340
Thousands upon thousands of domestic helpers
socialize on cardboard scattered across parks,

00:00:15.340 --> 00:00:19.980
walkways, and sidewalks on their one and only
day off.

00:00:19.980 --> 00:00:25.699
55% are from the Philippines — and virtually
all, women.

00:00:25.699 --> 00:00:30.789
A similar dynamic, but for men, plays out
on the high seas.

00:00:30.789 --> 00:00:37.010
Filipinos make up 1.4% of the world’s population
but roughly 30% of the world’s shipping

00:00:37.010 --> 00:00:38.280
crew.

00:00:38.280 --> 00:00:42.289
Take a cruise, for instance, and there’s
a very good chance you’ll have a Filipino

00:00:42.289 --> 00:00:44.250
maid, cook, or server.

00:00:44.250 --> 00:00:48.109
It’s not just low-skill work, either.

00:00:48.109 --> 00:00:52.809
One-fifth of California registered nurses
are from the Philippines, despite comprising

00:00:52.809 --> 00:00:56.680
only 4% of its population.

00:00:56.680 --> 00:01:03.949
On any given day, 10% of all living Filipinos
are employed overseas, 2.3 million of which

00:01:03.949 --> 00:01:06.540
are temporary workers.

00:01:06.540 --> 00:01:10.180
The reason may seem obvious: Money.

00:01:10.180 --> 00:01:15.000
The average nurse can make fifteen times more
working abroad.

00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:18.710
Even domestic helpers make far more than they
would at home.

00:01:18.710 --> 00:01:22.520
But why the Philippines, specifically?

00:01:22.520 --> 00:01:28.390
The country is neither the world’s largest,
fastest-growing, nor even poorest potential

00:01:28.390 --> 00:01:30.579
source of labor.

00:01:30.579 --> 00:01:35.329
Being the cheapest is a tough “competitive
advantage” to maintain in a world of seven

00:01:35.329 --> 00:01:37.110
billion people.

00:01:37.110 --> 00:01:41.250
There will always be someone, somewhere more
desperate.

00:01:41.250 --> 00:01:46.520
And yet, the Philippines has remained the
world’s go-to source of labor for decades,

00:01:46.520 --> 00:01:49.020
almost monopolizing entire industries.

00:01:49.020 --> 00:01:53.570
So, what’s the real reason?

00:01:53.570 --> 00:01:54.930
