the intuitive way with the link in the description.

00:02:01.690 --> 00:02:08.229
In 1973, the Middle East went on strike, cutting
off the world’s supply of oil.

00:02:08.229 --> 00:02:15.010
Between December 1973 and January 1974, the
global price of crude more than doubled, from

00:02:15.010 --> 00:02:18.170
26 to $61 per barrel.

00:02:18.170 --> 00:02:25.390
For the Philippines, which imported 80% of
its oil, this made a bad situation much worse.

00:02:25.390 --> 00:02:30.629
The Marcos government was already busy fighting
a Communist rebellion, had declared martial

00:02:30.629 --> 00:02:34.659
law, and was suffering a balance of trade
deficit when consumer prices went through

00:02:34.659 --> 00:02:37.860
the roof, and unemployment soon followed.

00:02:37.860 --> 00:02:44.650
But the rise in oil prices had a second unintended
consequence: the fundamental transformation

00:02:44.650 --> 00:02:47.680
of the global shipping industry.

00:02:47.680 --> 00:02:52.960
When the price of oil rose, demand for tankers
fell dramatically.

00:02:52.960 --> 00:02:58.970
But because a ship ordered today is only actually
delivered years down the road, their supply

00:02:58.970 --> 00:03:02.980
continued rising long after they were no longer
needed.

00:03:02.980 --> 00:03:08.180
As you can see, demand per tanker fell for
roughly the next ten years.

00:03:08.180 --> 00:03:13.909
And during this period of excess supply, shipping
became far less profitable.

00:03:13.909 --> 00:03:18.980
To cut costs, companies increasingly began
registering their ships in “Flag of Convenience”

00:03:18.980 --> 00:03:24.680
countries like Panama and Liberia, where they
could bypass national labor laws.

00:03:24.680 --> 00:03:30.319
Unions negotiated jobs for Japanese, Norwegian,
and Greek captains, but the bottom ranks were

00:03:30.319 --> 00:03:32.490
suddenly open to the world.

00:03:32.490 --> 00:03:38.540
And who better to fill them than the masses
of recently unemployed Filipinos.

00:03:38.540 --> 00:03:42.710
Shipping companies didn’t just get access
to a cheap, willing, and able workforce fluent

00:03:42.710 --> 00:03:50.140
in English, the international language of
the sea, but also their favorite kind: temporary.

00:03:50.140 --> 00:03:54.760
Migrant workers are a Capitalist’s vision
of globalization — perpetuating national

00:03:54.760 --> 00:03:58.970
borders when convenient, bypassing them when
not.

00:03:58.970 --> 00:04:03.950
By importing labor from abroad, you profit
from wage differentials between countries,

00:04:03.950 --> 00:04:08.370
but don’t have to worry about anti-immigrant
backlash.

00:04:08.370 --> 00:04:13.260
You can hire them as soon as you have a job
to be done, and fire them just as fast when

00:04:13.260 --> 00:04:17.220
it’s over, with none of the annoying paperwork.

00:04:17.220 --> 00:04:21.380
Virtually all the power in this relationship
lies with the employer, some of which even

00:04:21.380 --> 00:04:24.610
take away the passports of their workers.

00:04:24.610 --> 00:04:30.050
Until March of last year, for instance, domestic
helpers in Saudi Arabia needed their employer’s

00:04:30.050 --> 00:04:33.610
permission to leave the country.

00:04:33.610 --> 00:04:37.980
Needless to say, this serendipitous combination
of push — in the form of unemployment in

00:04:37.980 --> 00:04:42.480
the Philippines — and pull — in the form
of demand for cheap labor — produced an

00:04:42.480 --> 00:04:45.920
explosion in Filipino migrant workers.

00:04:45.920 --> 00:04:50.300
In 1960, 15% of all seafarers were Asian.

00:04:50.300 --> 00:04:57.000
By 1987, that number had more than quadrupled
to 67%.

00:04:57.000 --> 00:05:01.670
The next wave came from where the first originated:
the Middle East.

00:05:01.670 --> 00:05:05.760
The Gulf States had grand visions for what
to do with their new wealth but no one willing

00:05:05.760 --> 00:05:10.300
to actually build their underwater hotels
and giant empty picture frames.

00:05:10.300 --> 00:05:12.350
…That’s where foreign labor comes in.

00:05:12.350 --> 00:05:20.580
Today, 26% of Filipino migrants work in Saudi
Arabia, 14% in the UAE, 6 in Kuwait, and 5

00:05:20.580 --> 00:05:21.910
in Qatar.

00:05:21.910 --> 00:05:26.750
A significant portion of the rest work in
Hong Kong or Singapore as domestic helpers

00:05:26.750 --> 00:05:31.740
— doing the laundry, childcare, cooking,
cleaning, and everything in-between for middle-class

00:05:31.740 --> 00:05:33.550
households.

00:05:33.550 --> 00:05:37.270
Some families even have two or three.

00:05:37.270 --> 00:05:42.620
But this is really only half the answer to
our original question: why Filipinos came

00:05:42.620 --> 00:05:45.650
to dominate these specific labor niches.

00:05:45.650 --> 00:05:52.270
50 years ago the reason was, basically, coincidence
— timing, more than anything.

00:05:52.270 --> 00:05:57.340
But even if the Philippines was the cheapest
source of labor in 1970, it hasn’t remained

00:05:57.340 --> 00:06:00.930
that way for five consecutive decades.

00:06:00.930 --> 00:06:05.720
What began as a “temporary response” to
a unique set of circumstances, in other words,

00:06:05.720 --> 00:06:08.770
soon became a deliberate strategy.

00:06:08.770 --> 00:06:14.430
The money workers send home — called ‘remittances’
— has grown from a nice side gig to a pillar

00:06:14.430 --> 00:06:21.910
on which the entire national economy depends,
representing 10% of the country’s GDP.

00:06:21.910 --> 00:06:27.490
At $35 billion US Dollars a year, remittances
could be considered the Philippines’ second-largest

00:06:27.490 --> 00:06:33.690
export, just behind electronics, at $38 billion,
three times higher than third-place ‘machinery’,

00:06:33.690 --> 00:06:37.720
and thirteen times larger than fourth-place
copper.

00:06:37.720 --> 00:06:43.060
In the words of one former official, without
these overseas jobs reducing domestic unemployment,

00:06:43.060 --> 00:06:45.840
there would be revolution.

00:06:45.840 --> 00:06:50.650
And so, to sustain this flow of cash, the
Philippine government has become an unofficial

00:06:50.650 --> 00:06:52.990
broker of labor.

00:06:52.990 --> 00:06:58.240
When the President called herself the “CEO
of a global Philippine enterprise” in 2003,

00:06:58.240 --> 00:07:03.930
she wasn’t kidding — the state functions,
in many ways, like a corporation — one whose

00:07:03.930 --> 00:07:09.280
core business is selling one particular commodity
— labor.

00:07:09.280 --> 00:07:13.690
Government departments travel to trade shows,
negotiate with foreign countries, and sometimes

00:07:13.690 --> 00:07:15.880
even place labor directly.

00:07:15.880 --> 00:07:21.620
It even does marketing — producing posters,
videos, and ads reinforcing the perception

00:07:21.620 --> 00:07:26.080
of Filipinos as loyal, hard-working, and docile.

00:07:26.080 --> 00:07:31.280
Yet, despite all this, quote, “the State
does not promote overseas employment as a

00:07:31.280 --> 00:07:35.740
means to sustain economic growth and achieve
national development”.

00:07:35.740 --> 00:07:39.900
The government, in other words, is uneasy
about its participation in what some have

00:07:39.900 --> 00:07:42.770
called “legal human trafficking”.

00:07:42.770 --> 00:07:49.090
It takes credit for the inflow of remittances
but no responsibility when workers are abused.

00:07:49.090 --> 00:07:53.500
When they return home, workers are greeted
as “heroes”, not unlike members of the

00:07:53.500 --> 00:07:56.400
military in America.

00:07:56.400 --> 00:08:00.840
Sending money home is characterized as an
act of Nationalism, and each year the President

00:08:00.840 --> 00:08:04.360
celebrates “Migrant Workers Day”.

00:08:04.360 --> 00:08:07.660
But it’s not just the government reinforcing
this system.

00:08:07.660 --> 00:08:12.750
You might think Filipino migrant workers would
aggressively fight back against these stereotypes

00:08:12.750 --> 00:08:15.340
— and some do.

00:08:15.340 --> 00:08:19.210
But many don’t see themselves as being exploited.

00:08:19.210 --> 00:08:24.010
Despite high rates of abuse, harassment, and
harsh working conditions, overseas work is

00:08:24.010 --> 00:08:27.560
often viewed in a positive light, as an opportunity.

00:08:27.560 --> 00:08:32.000
…An opportunity that’s by no means guaranteed.

00:08:32.000 --> 00:08:38.010
Many see themselves as competing with Indonesians,
Pakistanis, and Nepalese.

00:08:38.010 --> 00:08:43.030
Reinforcing stereotypes, even about oneself,
is a powerful way of preserving these limited

00:08:43.030 --> 00:08:44.640
opportunities.

00:08:44.640 --> 00:08:52.280
But, more than anything else, what reinforces
this outflow of labor is economics.

00:08:52.280 --> 00:08:57.880
Imagine that a bundle of cash ten or fifteen
times the average local wage fell out of the

00:08:57.880 --> 00:09:03.200
sky and into every tenth house in your country,
each and every month.

00:09:03.200 --> 00:09:07.870
Why would that lucky 10% of the population
look for a job?

00:09:07.870 --> 00:09:12.620
Think about what kind of weird first, second,
and third-order effects this sudden airdrop

00:09:12.620 --> 00:09:15.150
of cash would have on the economy.

00:09:15.150 --> 00:09:21.430
Currency devaluation, inflation, lower real
incomes, and greater inequality between families

00:09:21.430 --> 00:09:25.140
receiving and not receiving remittances.

00:09:25.140 --> 00:09:30.630
The problem with treating labor as a resource,
in other words, is that it leads to the resource

00:09:30.630 --> 00:09:32.460
curse.

00:09:32.460 --> 00:09:34.820
What would these families buy with all that
money?

00:09:34.820 --> 00:09:40.650
A new car, an extra room in the house, or
even an extra house.

00:09:40.650 --> 00:09:44.690
Perhaps the most common purchase is a college
degree.

00:09:44.690 --> 00:09:49.400
But this is not quite the escape from poverty
and investment in the nation’s future that

00:09:49.400 --> 00:09:51.770
it may appear.

00:09:51.770 --> 00:09:57.140
Remittances are directly responsible for the
education of millions of young Filipinos.

00:09:57.140 --> 00:10:03.450
But they’re also responsible for the lack
of local demand for that very education.

00:10:03.450 --> 00:10:08.220
The end goal of a college degree, very often,
is to immigrate — often as a nurse — to

00:10:08.220 --> 00:10:13.320
the United States, in what has been called
“migrate to educate to migrate”

00:10:13.320 --> 00:10:20.210
First, a mother, father, or both, go overseas
as domestic helpers or sailors, sending whatever

00:10:20.210 --> 00:10:22.880
they can home to their children.

00:10:22.880 --> 00:10:27.380
Those children are often expected to use that
money to train as nurses, immigrate to the

00:10:27.380 --> 00:10:30.940
U.S., and take their turn sending remittances
home.

00:10:30.940 --> 00:10:36.970
In 2015, more Filipino nurses were employed
abroad than passed the nursing board exams

00:10:36.970 --> 00:10:39.060
at home.

00:10:39.060 --> 00:10:43.720
Until the practice was banned, nursing students
were so desperate to acquire the experience

00:10:43.720 --> 00:10:50.200
needed to go overseas that many paid hospitals
to allow them to work.

00:10:50.200 --> 00:10:54.830
For the lucky few whose parents sacrificed
their labor abroad, who themselves studied

00:10:54.830 --> 00:11:00.250
for years and years and years acquiring degrees,
and who have the incredible luck of entering

00:11:00.250 --> 00:11:05.400
the labor market at the right moment, this
is a powerful pathway to a better life for

00:11:05.400 --> 00:11:07.240
their families back home.

00:11:07.240 --> 00:11:13.180
But think about what’s really happening
here: Individuals are expected to work overseas

00:11:13.180 --> 00:11:18.270
just to pay for an education for the chance
of leaving, giving them the privilege of supporting

00:11:18.270 --> 00:11:20.040
their families.

00:11:20.040 --> 00:11:25.010
The state may take credit for this system,
but, for their families, these individual

00:11:25.010 --> 00:11:30.130
moms, dads, uncles, and aunts are fulfilling
the role of the state — providing basic

00:11:30.130 --> 00:11:33.290
things like food and education.

00:11:33.290 --> 00:11:38.690
And because it relies on an immense amount
of hard-work, sacrifice, and luck, this is

00:11:38.690 --> 00:11:41.930
not a sustainable, equitable process.

00:11:41.930 --> 00:11:48.260
It can’t really be scaled or systematized
and it relies on an increasingly tenuous assumption:

00:11:48.260 --> 00:11:53.820
that their labor and skills are appreciated
and welcome abroad.

00:11:53.820 --> 00:11:59.029
All it takes is a change of visa rules in
a foreign country or a pandemic to nullify

00:11:59.029 --> 00:12:02.440
all those years of sacrifice.

00:12:02.440 --> 00:12:06.450
Last year, for instance, the Philippine government
capped the number of nurses allowed to work

00:12:06.450 --> 00:12:13.410
overseas, forcing all but 5,000 to stay home,
risking their lives for very little money.

00:12:13.410 --> 00:12:16.040
So, what do they do?

00:12:16.040 --> 00:12:22.060
Many go back to school, in hopes that a second
or third-degree will make them more competitive.

00:12:22.060 --> 00:12:28.140
According to one study, 39% of working Filipinos
are “overeducated” for their jobs.

00:12:28.140 --> 00:12:31.660
Yet a quarter are “under-educated”.

00:12:31.660 --> 00:12:36.530
This is just one more way the traditional
system of education fails students — by

00:12:36.530 --> 00:12:40.660
preparing them for jobs that no longer exist.

00:12:40.660 --> 00:12:45.279
Learning should be flexible, interesting,
and based on practical universal skills like

00:12:45.279 --> 00:12:51.200
problem-solving, logic, and math — all things
which today’s sponsor, Brilliant, was designed

00:12:51.200 --> 00:12:52.520
to be.

